 Separators appear only between visible entries. Set `tab_bar_right_separator = ""` for direct concatenation. On a narrow tab row, the complete status area yields to the tabs and their controls.

+### Client-local footer
+
+`ui.footer` adds one separate, full-window footer row below the panes and sidebar. It leaves tab placement unchanged, inherits the client theme, and stays outside pane navigation, swaps, closes, and agent selection. An empty list disables it. Tiny terminals suppress the footer before it would displace the pane area. Modal dialogs may temporarily cover it.
+
+```toml
+[ui]
+footer = [
+  { type = "text", text = "Useful shortcuts" },
+  { type = "command", command = "my-hints-helper", interval_seconds = 5, timeout_seconds = 2 },
+]
+footer_separator = " · "
+```
+
+Footer commands run on the client machine only when a connected Local endpoint is selected. They receive `HERDR_FOOTER_WIDTH` (available columns), `HERDR_FOOTER_LOCAL=1`, the selected Local API socket in `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH`, `HERDR_BIN_PATH`, `HERDR_CONFIG_PATH`, and active workspace, tab and pane IDs. Commands are suppressed on SSH endpoints or while disconnected; literal text still renders. They run independently of rendering with no overlap per entry, bounded output, timeouts, and process-group cleanup on configuration reload or client exit. Herdr uses the last successful output line, removes terminal controls, limits it to 4096 characters, and clips the row by display width without wrapping. Failures clear the corresponding text without changing the reserved row. At most 16 entries are supported, with intervals 1–31536000 seconds and timeouts 1–3600 seconds.
+
+`herdr --client-capabilities` prints supported client feature versions as JSON; `{"footer":1}` advertises this footer configuration. This client-local feature needs no endpoint protocol change and works with existing compatible servers.
+
 ### Outer terminal window title

 Herdr emulates the terminals in its panes, so an `OSC 0`/`OSC 2` title written inside a pane stops at Herdr. Herdr writes its own title to the terminal it runs in, which is what window managers and terminal tab bars read:
